-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Precision:** Logarithms of values close to 0 can cause instability. Use `log(1.0 - r)` where `r` is in `[0, 1)` to avoid `log(0)` [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Boundary Conditions:** Rays missing the volume must be handled explicitly to avoid sampling errors [S2, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Majorant Selection:** If $\sigma_{maj}$ is too close to the actual extinction, the null-collision method becomes inefficient (many null collisions). If too large, variance increases [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Accumulation:** Without temporal accumulation, the result will be noisy. The accumulation formula requires a frame counter [S2, unofficial].
-
-## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot B \cdot S)$ where $N$ is pixels, $B$ is max bounces, $S$ is steps per bounce. Each step involves a random number generation and a texture lookup [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for volume data, $O(P)$ for photon buffers if using photon mapping variant [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Converges slowly due to stochastic nature, requiring multiple frames for low noise [S2, unofficial]. GPU implementation using compute shaders allows parallel photon tracing [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles complex lighting and participating media naturally; avoids uniform sampling artifacts; adaptive to density variations [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Noisy output; requires accumulation for convergence; sensitive to majorant selection [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When high-quality scattering effects are needed and uniform sampling is insufficient or too expensive [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When real-time performance without accumulation is required [S2, unofficial].
-
-## Background
-Monte Carlo Volume Rendering originated as an alternative to uniform sampling to reduce artifacts in homogeneous materials [S1, unofficial]. It was implemented in various research projects and open-source renderers, such as the `volumetric-ray-casting` library by rokcej and the `vpt` (Volume Processing Toolkit) by terier [S2, S3, unofficial]. The method leverages GPU compute shaders for parallel photon tracing [S2, unofficial].
